# Post–Big-Prompt Cleanup

After finishing a **large** implementation (multi-file feature, new domain, major UI redesign, roadmap phase, or any prompt that changed migrations, seeders, routes, Vite entries, or many views), run this wrap-up before declaring done.

## Required sequence (PowerShell)

Use `;` between commands (not `&&`):

```powershell
php artisan migrate --no-interaction --force
php artisan db:seed --no-interaction --force
php artisan optimize
npm run build
```

Then ensure the Vite dev server is running for local UI work:

```powershell
npm run dev
```

- If `npm run dev` is **already** running in a terminal, do not start a second one.
- If only a one-shot verify is needed and `npm run dev` is already up, `npm run build` alone is enough for production assets; still run migrate/seed/optimize when schema or PHP caches changed.

## When this applies

- New migrations, models, permissions, seeders
- New Vite entrypoints (`vite.config.js`) or large Blade/CSS/JS changes
- Multi-phase / “implement the plan” / full-page redesign prompts

## When to skip

- Tiny one-file edits, copy tweaks, or questions with no code changes
- Read-only investigation

## Notes

- Prefer `--no-interaction` / `--force` on Artisan so commands do not hang.
- `db:seed` refreshes seeded reference data (permissions/users/etc.); do not invent a custom wipe of business data.
- After `php artisan optimize`, remember cached config can affect tests — if Feature tests start hitting the wrong DB, run `php artisan config:clear` before `php artisan test`.
